---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Lesson 11: Smart Servo Control - C00lnerd Tutoring">
    <div style="max-width: 1000px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem;">
        
        <!-- Header -->
        <div style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #667eea 0%, #764ba2 100%); color: white; padding: 3rem; border-radius: 15px; margin-bottom: 2rem; text-align: center;">
            <h1 style="font-size: 2.5rem; font-weight: bold; margin: 0 0 1rem 0;">Lesson 11: Smart Servo Control</h1>
            <p style="font-size: 1.2rem; margin: 0 0 1rem 0; opacity: 0.9;">Smooth non-blocking motion and serial commands on two SG90 servos</p>
            <div style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.1); padding: 1rem; border-radius: 8px; margin-top: 1.5rem;">
                <p style="margin: 0; font-weight: 600;">🦾 Everything you build today is exactly what the robot arm will run on — no printed parts needed yet</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Learning Objectives -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">🎯 Learning Objectives</h2>
            
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2rem;">
                <div>
                    <h3 style="color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Lesson Goals:</h3>
                    <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                        <li>• Replace <code>delay()</code> with non-blocking <code>millis()</code> timing</li>
                        <li>• Ramp two servos smoothly to target angles at the same time</li>
                        <li>• Build a Serial Monitor command interface (<code>B90</code>, <code>E45</code>, <code>HOME</code>, <code>POSE n</code>)</li>
                        <li>• Store preset poses using the <code>struct</code> arrays from Lesson 9.5</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3 style="color: #2d3748; font-size: 1.3rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Skills Developed:</h3>
                    <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                        <li>• Timing patterns used in every real robot program</li>
                        <li>• Serial input parsing and simple text commands</li>
                        <li>• Coordinated motion and safe angle limits</li>
                        <li>• Software that transfers straight onto the robot arm later</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Section 1: Parts Inventory -->
        <div style="background: white; padding: 2rem; border-radius: 15px; box-shadow: 0 4px 6px rgba(0,0,0,0.1); margin-bottom: 2rem;">
            <h2 style="color: #667eea; font-size: 1.8rem; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1.5rem;">📦 Section 1: Parts Inventory</h2>
            
            <div style="display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 2rem;">
                <div style="background: #e6fffa; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Electronic Components:</h4>
                    <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                        <li>• Arduino Uno microcontroller</li>
                        <li>• 2x SG90 micro servos (call them "base" and "elbow" — they stay loose on your desk)</li>
                        <li>• Breadboard (or expansion board) + jumper wires</li>
                        <li>• Push button (A2 to GND, INPUT_PULLUP)</li>
                        <li>• Potentiometer (A0) for angle control</li>
                        <li>• Buzzer (optional) for command feedback</li>
                        <li>• USB cable (also used for the Serial Monitor commands)</li>
                    </ul>
                </div>
                <div style="background: #fff7ed; padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px;">
                    <h4 style="color: #2d3748; font-weight: bold; margin-bottom: 1rem;">Mechanical Parts:</h4>
                    <ul style="list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0; color: #4a5568; line-height: 1.8;">
                        <li>• <strong>None required this week!</strong> The servos can sit loose on the table</li>
                        <li>• (Optional) Tape or poster tack to keep servo bodies from wandering</li>
                        <li>• (Optional) Paperclip or pointer taped to a servo horn to make motion easier to see</li>
                    </ul>
                    <p style="color: #4a5568; margin-top: 1rem; line-height: 1.6; font-size: 0.95rem;">
                        The 3D printed arm parts are still being designed. When they arrive, this exact program becomes the arm's control software — that's why we label the servos "base" and "elbow" now.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>

